The Type-1 advantage is

\mathbf{Adv}^{\mathrm{bind}}_{\mathcal{A}}(\lambda,\rho)
=\Pr\!\left[\mathbf{Exp}^{\mathrm{bind}}_{\mathcal{A}}(\lambda,\rho)=1\right].

The winning condition is deliberately narrower than “the adversary obtains a token.” A token obtained through a corrupt factor is a Type-1 win only if the factor corruption is the causal route; a token obtained through a cryptographically forged attestation is a Type-1 win irrespective of factor state. The \delta_c^{\mathrm{factor}}=0 condition isolates the possession-insufficiency claim from the factor-corruption claim.

Appendix C. Sequence of Games and Concrete Bound

Game 0 is \mathbf{Exp}^{\mathrm{bind}}_{\mathcal{A}}.

Game 1 (ideal attestation). Replace \sigma_I by an ideal unforgeable primitive. Any distinguishing event yields a reduction \mathcal{B}_1 against EUF-CMA:

\bigl|\Pr[G_0]-\Pr[G_1]\bigr|
\le \mathbf{Adv}^{\mathrm{euf\text{-}cma}}_{\mathcal{B}_1}(\lambda).

Game 2 (fresh, single-use nonces). Replace every n_P by an ideal uniform value and enforce single-use consumption of both nonces. The distinguishing event is a collision or reuse among q_B binding queries:

\bigl|\Pr[G_1]-\Pr[G_2]\bigr|
\le \frac{q_B^2}{2^{|\mathcal{N}|}}.

Game 3 (authenticated channel and ideal session table). Replace the P–I channel by an ideal authenticated channel and the issuer session table by an ideal injective map from accepted tuples (u,c,d,s,n_P,n_I) to issuer events. The distinguishing advantage is bounded by a reduction \mathcal{B}_3:

\bigl|\Pr[G_2]-\Pr[G_3]\bigr|
\le \mathbf{Adv}^{\mathrm{chan+table}}_{\mathcal{B}_3}(\lambda).

The property in the reduction is session-table correctness: no two distinct accepted tuples map to the same issuer event. This is a correctness property of the issuer’s state machine, not the injective-agreement conclusion. If no reduction to the authenticated-channel and table primitives can be constructed, the term must be stated as an assumption.

Game 4 (factor-only winning route). In G_3, the sole remaining winning strategy is factor corruption on at least one queried credential followed by use before the experiment ends. Let \mathcal{Q}_F be the set of distinct credentials on which \mathcal{A} queries \mathrm{CorruptFactor}. Under independence across credentials,

\Pr[G_3]\le 1-\prod_{c\in\mathcal{Q}_F}(1-\rho_c),

and for small \rho_c the right-hand side is approximately \sum_{c\in\mathcal{Q}_F}\rho_c.

Combining,

\mathbf{Adv}^{\mathrm{bind}}_{\mathcal{A}}(\lambda,\rho)
\le \mathbf{Adv}^{\mathrm{euf\text{-}cma}}_{\mathcal{B}_1}(\lambda)
+ \frac{q_B^2}{2^{|\mathcal{N}|}}
+ \mathbf{Adv}^{\mathrm{chan+table}}_{\mathcal{B}_3}(\lambda)
+ \left(1-\prod_{c\in\mathcal{Q}_F}(1-\rho_c)\right).

The first three terms are asymptotic in \lambda. The fourth is not: SIM-swap, message redirection, call-centre social engineering, and authenticator rebinding are not parameterised by a cryptographic security parameter. The bound is therefore a mixed asymptotic–empirical statement, and its diagnostic value lies in identifying which term dominates in a given deployment. If the factor term dominates, hardening \sigma_I does not address the binding risk; the relevant control is authentication-channel security. If the channel-and-table term dominates, the relevant control is session binding and attestation consumption.

Appendix D. Type-2 Post-Binding Game

Experiment \mathbf{Exp}^{\mathrm{postbind}}_{\mathcal{A}}(\lambda,\rho_2).

1. The challenger runs a legitimate binding producing an accepted token t for account u.
2. \mathcal{A} receives \mathrm{CompromisePlatformAccount}(u), granting control of u.
3. After compromise, \mathcal{A} may initiate transactions through P.
4. For each post-binding transaction, P outputs A_2\in\{\mathrm{allow},\mathrm{challenge},\mathrm{suspend}\}.
5. The experiment outputs 1 iff A_2=\mathrm{allow} on a transaction that the legitimate holder did not authorize.

The Type-2 advantage is

\mathbf{Adv}^{\mathrm{postbind}}_{\mathcal{A}}(\lambda,\rho_2)
=\Pr\!\left[A_2=\mathrm{allow}\mid\text{account compromised, transaction unauthorized by holder}\right],

where \rho_2 is the empirical rate of post-binding compromise that evades existing account-takeover controls.

The Type-2 game is not a corollary of the Type-1 bound. A Type-1 win produces an unauthorized binding; a Type-2 win produces unauthorized use of a previously authorized binding. The controls differ: Type-1 is addressed by attestation binding and issuance ordering; Type-2 is addressed by fresh account authentication and step-up credential proof on sensitive post-binding actions.

Appendix E. Lifecycle State Machine and Revocation Terminality

State space.

Q=\{\mathrm{Pending},\mathrm{Verified},\mathrm{Active},
\mathrm{Suspended}_{\mathrm{account}},
\mathrm{Suspended}_{\mathrm{credential}},
\mathrm{Suspended}_{\mathrm{device}},
\mathrm{Revoked}\}.

Permitted transitions (excerpt).

· \mathrm{Active}\to\mathrm{Suspended}_{\mathrm{account}} on account compromise;
· \mathrm{Active}\to\mathrm{Suspended}_{\mathrm{credential}} on credential compromise;
· \mathrm{Active}\to\mathrm{Suspended}_{\mathrm{device}} on device loss;
· re-entry from each suspended state requires the cause-specific recovery condition: account recovery and fresh platform authentication for \mathrm{Suspended}_{\mathrm{account}}; fresh issuer attestation for \mathrm{Suspended}_{\mathrm{credential}}; device re-enrollment for \mathrm{Suspended}_{\mathrm{device}};
· \mathrm{Revoked} is absorbing.

Revocation terminality (specification invariant). For every reachable state q, if q=\mathrm{Revoked}, then the only transition is the self-loop \mathrm{Revoked}\to\mathrm{Revoked}.

This is stated as an inductive invariant over the specification, not as a theorem about implementations. Implementation-level enforcement requires a monotonic revocation-epoch counter checked on every token use, together with an argument that cached token state cannot bypass the epoch check. The specification proof and the implementation audit are distinct obligations; satisfying the former does not discharge the latter.

The EMVCo Payment Tokenisation Specification provides real-time and continuous lifecycle management (issue, suspend, resume, delete, activate, expiry), with lifecycle events triggerable by different parties. CABIM’s state space refines that lifecycle by adding cause-specific suspension states and re-entry conditions. The refinement is not a claim that EMVCo lifecycle management is absent; it is a claim that a single \mathrm{Suspended} state does not encode the recovery condition, and that the recovery condition determines whether a suspended binding may safely return to \mathrm{Active}.

Appendix F. Competing-Duration Exposure Model

Let

T_{\mathrm{rev}}=T_{\mathrm{detect}}+T_{\mathrm{prop}},

with propagation counted once inside the revocation interval. Residual expected loss is

\mathbb{E}[L]
=\ell\cdot\Pr\!\left[T_{\mathrm{rev}}>T_{\mathrm{monetize}}\right]
=\ell\cdot\mathbb{E}\!\left[S_{\mathrm{rev}}(T_{\mathrm{monetize}})\right],

where S_{\mathrm{rev}}(t)=\Pr[T_{\mathrm{rev}}>t].

This is a race between a revocation duration and an adversary-controlled monetization duration, not a competing-risk model in the survival-analysis sense of mutually exclusive event types. The distinction matters for estimation: T_{\mathrm{monetize}} is chosen by the adversary and may be deliberately right-shifted, so an empirical estimate of its distribution from observed fraud must account for the adversary’s selection. Immediate post-provisioning monitoring observes only the early portion of the T_{\mathrm{monetize}} distribution and therefore systematically underestimates the exposure from delayed monetization.

Visa’s operational estimates provide scale for \ell and for the prior over T_{\mathrm{monetize}}: token provisioning fraud losses are estimated at $809 million globally in 2025, up from $450 million in 2022 (Visa MIS; based on Visa Product Brands, VisaNet Clearing and Settlement and Fraud Reporting as of 25 July 2025). These are moving targets, not stationary parameters. They calibrate the order of magnitude of \ell and the direction of change in the adversary’s provisioning capability; they do not fix the distributions required for a deployment-specific exposure calculation.

Appendix G. Decision Rule and Feasibility

Let p_t(e)=\Pr[H_1\mid X(e),\mathcal{F}_t] be the decision-time calibrated probability. The unconstrained Bayes rule rejects when

p_t\cdot C_{\mathrm{FN}}>(1-p_t)\cdot C_{\mathrm{FP}},

equivalently when p_t>p^* with

p^*=\frac{C_{\mathrm{FP}}}{C_{\mathrm{FN}}+C_{\mathrm{FP}}}.

The constrained program is

\pi^*=\arg\min_{\pi}\mathbb{E}[C(\pi)]
\quad\text{s.t.}\quad
\Pr[\text{unauthorized binding}\mid\pi]\le\delta_b,\;
\Pr[\text{false positive}\mid\pi]\le\delta_{\mathrm{FP}}.

The constraint set must be non-empty. If no policy simultaneously satisfies both bounds, the problem is over-constrained and either a bound must be relaxed or a richer feature distribution acquired. The statement that “reject everything is optimal” is false when false-decline cost is included in C(\pi): rejecting everything incurs C_{\mathrm{FP}} on every legitimate attempt and is generally dominated. The correct feasibility condition is the existence of an operating point achieving both \delta_b and \delta_{\mathrm{FP}}, not the absence of a false-positive constraint.

All calibration and performance claims are stated relative to \mathcal{F}_t. A policy that is Bayes-optimal under \mathcal{F}_t need not be optimal under eventual ground-truth labels. The framework does not claim global optimality; it claims decision-time optimality given the information available when the decision is made.

Appendix H. Identification Strategies for the Six Hypotheses

H1. Proof obligation. If any of the three reductions (EUF-CMA, nonce freshness, authenticated channel and session table) fails to construct, the corresponding term is restated as an assumption and H1 is falsified for that mechanism. H1 is not an experiment.

H2 (assurance monotonicity). The current NIST provision permits a lower-assurance session to bind a higher-assurance authenticator: SP 800-63B-4 requires authentication at the lower of the maximum AAL currently available in the subscriber account and the maximum AAL at which the new authenticator will be used. Sakimura’s analysis identifies the resulting takeover path and proposes binding at an AAL equal to or higher than the new authenticator’s AAL, registration at the current session’s AAL, or repeated identity proofing. CABIM adopts the equal-or-higher rule as a proposed invariant, not as a description of current NIST requirements. Testing H2 requires an instrument or a discontinuity in the assurance-assignment rule, because assurance is assigned by risk and an unadjusted comparison of takeover rates by assurance level is confounded by design.

H3 (graph-plus-feature detection). Both detectors must be evaluated under the same delay-and-censoring adjustment. A comparison on naively labeled data does not test the hypothesis, because the impairment multipliers identified in the regret-bound literature enter multiplicatively and affect both detectors asymmetrically. The comparison requires a held-out attack set and a fixed decision-time false-positive budget.

H4 (issuance ordering). Authentication policy and population must be held fixed. A comparison against a system that also changed authentication policy does not identify the ordering effect. The appropriate design is a staged rollout with shadow scoring, in which the issue-then-verify and verify-then-issue sequences are applied to comparable populations under the same issuer policy.

H5 (revocation latency). Estimate S_{\mathrm{rev}} and the distribution of T_{\mathrm{monetize}} on a held-out period, and compare predicted loss under the race model to observed loss and to a baseline that uses token validity alone. The estimate of T_{\mathrm{monetize}} must account for adversary selection; an estimate based only on observed fraud in the early post-provisioning window is biased downward.

H6 (label-impairment bias). On the same period, compute detector performance with and without a delay-and-censoring model. The optimistic bias must increase with the impairment multipliers. The reference formalisation is Dhama’s minimax regret bound for delayed, censored, corrupted, and counterfactually missing feedback, which shows that these impairments enter multiplicatively in the denominator of the achievable learning rate and that improving issuer reporting quality or reducing censorship can reduce the regret floor more than increasing model complexity. H6 is falsified if the bias does not increase with impairment, or if a model estimated on naively treated labels matches the adjusted estimate within the pre-registered tolerance.

The EBA position in Q&A 2021_6145 provides a jurisdiction-specific reference point for the binding event: unlocking a mobile phone with biometrics or a PIN is not a valid SCA element for adding a payment card to a digital wallet unless the screen-locking mechanism is under the issuer’s control or the unlocking has been tied to the payer through prior SCA. That position is EU law and does not directly govern U.S. platform payment rails. CABIM uses it as evidence that the binding event is recognised as an authentication event requiring its own evidentiary basis, not as a portable legal requirement. Similarly, the FIDO-in-3DS guidance published by EMVCo (28 October 2020) and the ProVerif-verified issuer-managed 3DS–FIDO framework (IEEE, 16 November 2026) provide the closest protocol-level references; the 2026 framework’s revocation mechanism and symbolic verification are integrated into the comparison, not claimed as CABIM results.

